+# Keep sessions in process memory
+
+For whoever changes login, deploys or the session store. Sessions live in the web app's process memory because
+volunteers requested faster requests without a Postgres session read.
+
+## Context
+
+In 2023 larder moved from one server to two behind a load balancer, and sessions stayed in each server's memory.
+Members were logged out whenever a request reached the other server, and every deploy logged out everyone; the pantry
+volunteers stopped deploying during opening hours. Sessions moved to Postgres to survive deploys and serve requests
+on either server without adding another service.
+
+Volunteers now want the app faster and requested a process-local dictionary instead of a Postgres read on every
+request. This change prioritizes removing that database call over preserving sessions across servers and restarts.
+No measurement of the effect on total request latency has been supplied.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Sticky sessions at the load balancer**: can keep a member on one server, but cannot preserve sessions across a
+  restart and is not configured by this change.
+- **Redis as a session store**: fast and common; lost on the second force, a service the volunteers would run only
+  for sessions.
+- **Postgres**: chosen previously for shared, durable sessions; replaced to remove the session query from requests.
+- **A dictionary in each web app process**: chosen to remove database session access without adding a service.
+
+## Decision
+
+Sessions live in a dictionary in each web app process. The shared `sessions` instance in
+[`larder/session_store.py`](../../larder/session_store.py) provides the store without a database connection.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Session reads and writes no longer query Postgres. A request reaching another process cannot find the session, and
+a restart or deploy discards every session in that process. Members may be logged out and lose session-held pantry
+orders. Sticky routing can reduce cross-process misses but cannot prevent restart losses. Session memory is local
+and grows with stored sessions; the store does not evict expired entries. End-to-end speed improvements remain
+unmeasured.
